<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">National Policy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1879</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The National Policy was the economic programme introduced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-a-macdonald">John A. Macdonald</a>'s Conservative government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> in 1879. It combined high <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protective-tariff">protective tariffs</a> on imported manufactures, the building of the Canadian Pacific Railway and settlement of the western prairies, so that the industries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> would supply a national market. Tariff protection remained central to Canadian policy for decades.</p></div>
